The '''History of Guajicutea (1547–1555)''' covers the period from the Burawi conquest of the [[Aruanãyára Empire|Aruanãyára]] in full force through the First Guaji Civil War and the resulting political reorganization that brought [[Volisania]] into a direct presence on Guajicutea. As Burawa withdrew military forces to support its northern war, the Lord of [[Waribá]] — [[Buka Wariban]] — conspired to usurp the throne, leading to the assassination of [[Petro I of Guajicutea|Boqor Petros I]]'s heir and a four-month civil war. The conflict drew in both Burawa and Volisania, ending with Petros's second son [[Dawit I of Guajicutea|Boqor Dawit I]] on the throne, significant concessions to Volisania, and the establishment of a Volisanian religious and military presence on the island alongside a reduced Burawi contingent.

== The Aruanãyára war and the Burawi withdrawal ==

In 1547, Burawa launched its conquest of the [[Aruanãyára Empire|Aruanãyára]] in full force — the war that [[Yabí Tabá]] had predicted in his 1535 papers. The initial impact on Guajicutea was limited: an uptick in Guaji warriors hired to fight in Aruanãyára, but no direct change to the island's administration.

By 1553, the escalation of the war in the north forced Burawa to recall two of its three battalions from Guajicutea, leaving a skeleton force to support the garrison at [[Waribá]]. The withdrawal reduced Burawi military capacity on the island to a level that could maintain the show of force but could not respond to a major crisis.

== The Second Capuyaquiran War (1538–1548) ==

While Burawa focused on the Aruanãyára, Volisania expanded its control over the western Capuyaquiran. The Second Capuyaquiran War (1538–1548) saw Volisania annex the Lesser Capuyaquiran and mainland territories of the [[Tapuya Confederacy|Tapuya]], taking most of the remaining islands west of Guajicutea. The [[Yaruba]] islands were excluded — they had signed an alliance with Burawa in 1535 and were off-limits to Volisanian expansion.

The war shifted the balance of power in the Capuyaquiran. Volisania now controlled most of the western archipelago, while Burawa's influence was concentrated in the east and on Guajicutea. The [[Abyala Charter]], signed in 1537, had divided the mainland of [[Abyala]] between the two powers but left the islands unregulated — creating the conditions for Volisanian expansion.

== The conspiracy of Buka Wariban ==

As Burawi forces withdrew, the Lord of [[Waribá]] — [[Buka Wariban]] — began conspiring with other southern and western lords to usurp the throne. [[Petro I of Guajicutea|Boqor Petros I]] was aging and in declining health, and had expressed reluctance about his son's succession. Buka Wariban saw the weakness as an opportunity.

The conspirators worked with the Volisanian colonial government in the [[Balboa]] colony to secure support for the usurpation. Volisania, which had signed the 1537 agreement of understanding with Guajicutea and had been cultivating influence on the island, was willing to support a change of regime that would shift Guajicutea away from Burawi influence. The relationship between Burawa and Volisania at the time was a cordial but strenuous alliance — the two powers were cooperating in the Aruanãyára war while competing for colonial influence in the Capuyaquiran.

== Death of Petros I and the assassination (1554) ==

Boqor Petros I died in 1554. His heir — the first son — was crowned [[Petro II of Guajicutea|Boqor Petro II]] and assassinated during the coronation ceremony by members of his own court. The Burawi Emissary was in attendance at the coronation. The assassination was violent and chaotic, and the Emissary was forced to flee the island along with his soldier cohort, escaping on [[Sarta]] vessels.

== First Guaji Civil War (1554) ==

{{main|First Guaji Civil War}}

The assassination triggered the First Guaji Civil War, fought between the southern and western lords who supported the conspiracy and the eastern lords and lords near Waribá who remained loyal to the royal line. The war lasted four months and resulted in the deaths of thousands of Guaji peasants.

Buka Wariban moved quickly to consolidate control. He seized the Dabra mission and imprisoned the Burawi religious staff. The Emissary's fort near the port was besieged but held for two weeks. Meanwhile, Petros's second son, Dawit, declared himself king, presenting a rival claim to the throne.

Volisania intervened on the conspirators' side, sending a ship of weapons to Buka Wariban and impressing Guaji ships into service. [[New Burawa]], receiving news of the crisis a week after the assassination, dispatched a fleet to Waribá to support the legitimate heir. The fleet laid siege to the port, but Burawa was forced to recall the ships after a month — the Aruanãyára war demanded the naval resources — and requested Volisanian assistance.

Volisania seized the opportunity. A large Volisanian fleet was dispatched to stabilize the situation, giving Volisania the pretext for direct military intervention that it had been seeking.

== Resolution ==

The civil war ended after four months with a settlement that favored Volisanian interests. Against Buka Wariban's wishes, Dawit was given the throne as [[Dawit I of Guajicutea|Boqor Dawit I]]. In exchange, Dawit I granted Volisania significant concessions, and Burawa allowed Volisania to take all islands west of Guajicutea. The lord of Waribá's conspiracy had failed, but Volisania emerged as the dominant external power on the island.

== Religious and political reorganization ==

The end of the civil war produced a new political and religious landscape on Guajicutea. Volisania followed the Petran Catholic Church — a tradition in communion with the Burawi church but distinct in its liturgy and organization. The Burawi church was allowed to remain, but Volisania established its own mission in the inland capital of [[Tabakurí]] and set up a fort at [[Barikú]] on the west coast.

Burawi forces were reduced to a single minor battalion, and a new Emissary was appointed. Burawi influence was now concentrated primarily in the Waribá area, while Volisania held the capital and the western port. The arrangement reflected the new balance of power: Burawa, still focused on the Aruanãyára war, could not maintain its former presence on Guajicutea and had ceded ground to Volisania.

